# Workspace-root environment for LOCAL DEVELOPMENT. Copy to `.env` (gitignored).
#
# Two kinds of value live here and nothing else:
#
#  1. Machine-specific compose port overrides. `docker compose` reads this file automatically
#     because the `moon run api:db-*` tasks run from the workspace root.
#  2. The SHARED warehouse + object-store settings every provider sync service needs. Each
#     service's moon tasks read `/.env` first and then its own `services/*/.env`, so a service
#     file holds only its own API credentials.
#
# Everything else belongs to one app or service: see `apps/*/.env.example` and
# `services/*/.env.example`. Do not put WorkOS / Seccl / SECRETS_MASTER_KEY here —
# local compose interpolates this file even for db-up. Those live in
# `apps/*/.env.local` and `services/*/.env`. Deployment configuration is
# `docker-compose.yaml` plus `infrastructure/README.md`.

# ── Local compose ports ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Defaults are 5432 / 9000 / 9001. Override when something else on the machine has them.
DB_PORT=5432
RUSTFS_PORT=9000
RUSTFS_CONSOLE_PORT=9001

# ── Shared warehouse + object store ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE value per fact. Each service used to ask for its own copy (`SECCL_WAREHOUSE_DSN`,
# `ML_S3_ENDPOINT`, `SEI_S3_ENDPOINT`, …) — fifteen variables for four facts, and three places to
# update when RUSTFS_PORT changes. Worth removing because of how the mistake fails, not because it
# is untidy: a wrong endpoint does not raise, the parquet lands somewhere the reader never looks,
# and it presents as "the catalogue is empty" — indistinguishable from "this provider has no data".
#
# A service `.env` can still override any of these, for a provider genuinely pointed elsewhere.

# Host-native only (`localhost`). Compose warehouse block uses `host=db` / `rustfs:9000`.
#
# lake_writer on the local `datalakes` DB (created by migrations/bootstrap/03-datalakes.sql).
DATALAKE_WRITER_DSN="dbname=datalakes host=localhost port=5432 user=lake_writer password=lake_writer"
# The same DB as the read-only lake_reader role — every READER of a provider's core catalogue
# uses this one (apps/api's app/lake, and services/integrations/intelliflo's shared/lake.py).
# Never lake_writer: a reader has no business being able to write another service's warehouse.
DATALAKE_CATALOG_DSN="dbname=datalakes host=localhost port=5432 user=lake_reader password=lake_reader"
# book_writer on the PORTALS DB — where a provider's pipeline lands the tenant's slice and then
# merges it into the book (docs/provider-pipeline.md). Shared, and here rather than in a service
# file for the same reason as the line above: all three provider services need it, and one value
# per fact is the rule this file exists to keep.
#
# `book_writer`, NOT `app` and NOT `service_role`. It reaches the merged relations, the reject
# ledger and three read-only tables, and nothing else — `service_role` has ALL on auth.users,
# documents.*, aml.*, flows.* and storage.*, so a compromised daemon could read a client's identity
# documents or a firm's screening outcomes (migration 0062). BYPASSRLS is unavoidable for a writer
# that spans tenants and is stated plainly there rather than argued away.
#
# libpq KEYWORD form, not a URL: interlace interpolates it into `postgres:${BOOK_WRITER_DSN}` for
# DuckDB's postgres extension, which takes libpq. Left unset, interlace keeps the literal
# `${BOOK_WRITER_DSN}` and fails loudly on connect rather than writing somewhere surprising, and
# `book_sink` registers no tenant models at all (it logs why — an unset DSN otherwise reads as a
# daemon with no tenants).
BOOK_WRITER_DSN="dbname=portals host=localhost port=5432 user=book_writer password=book_writer"
# The core bucket: raw/staged provider data plus every DuckLake parquet zone.
S3_CORE_BUCKET=platforms
S3_INTEGRATIONS_BUCKET=integrations
# host[:port], NO scheme — the form the DuckDB/interlace secret takes. Keep the port in step with
# RUSTFS_PORT above. (The API wants the URL form instead: S3_ENDPOINT_URL in apps/api/.env.local.)
S3_ENDPOINT_HOST=localhost:9000
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=rustfsadmin
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=rustfsadmin

# Which Postgres the render worker connects to (docker-compose.local.yaml → the `render` service).
# Leave unset for a single checkout: the default `db` is the alias on the shared `graphene-dev`
# network. Set it to a CONTAINER NAME (e.g. graphene-db) when you have two checkouts attached to
# that network — both would otherwise answer to `db`, and the worker would poll whichever the
# resolver picked, leaving documents queued for ever or failing them as "template version missing".
#RENDER_DB_HOST=graphene-db
